  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nCurrent conditions/Tonight:\n\nSurface high pressure has set up residency over the Northland and \nwill be a mainstay through the weekend and early next week. \nSatellite shows the morning stratus burning off across northern MN \nwith ongoing lake effect snow showers continuing across the UP and \nadvecting west. Our 850 mb temps still remain around -8C which leads \nto lake induced instability of around 200 J/kg. Current surface \ndewpoint depressions over the South Shore are around 8-10F which is \ntoo dry for precipitation. But upstream where some greenish hues can \nbe depicted in the day cloud phase RGB show dewpoint \ndepressions are on the order of 1-3F with ongoing snow. This \nactivity should cross over into Iron and Ashland late this \nafternoon before dry synoptic air and a wind shift shuts the \nprocess down this evening. Minimal accumulation expected. \n\nWeekend - Mid Week:\n\nQuiet weather period as upper level ridge sets up over the Upper \nMidwest through an extended period. Temperatures will gradually be \non the upwards trend with highs ascending into the 50s by Wednesday. \nFor context, normal highs for this time of the year are in the upper \n30s. Current NBM guidance even has some record breaking warm lows \nfor Thursday with values in the low 40s. \n\nEnd of next work week:\n\nWeather pattern shift does not look to arrive until late Wednesday \ninto Thursday as troughing in the west finally pushes the upper \nlevel ridge out of the region. Forecast trends continue to slow the \nadvancement of these synoptic features as well as push the \nassociated surface low farther north. As such, NBM PoPs \ncontinue to be delayed with 30% chances returning late \nWednesday into Thursday. Given the strong dry air in place and \nthe inconsistency of model to model runs confidence remains low \nin the timing and placement of rain returning to the Northland.",
